      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks — I agree the \"one change split into two repo patches\" pattern is something to avoid.\n\nThat said, I\u0027d still prefer to keep this in its own repo, for three reasons:\n\n1. Entity Manager\u0027s role in this design is only to provide the platform configuration. ccid is a *consumer* of xyz.openbmc_project.Configuration.*, in exactly the same position as bmc-code-mgmt, dbus-sensors, pldmd and mctpd. bmc-code-mgmt\u0027s fw-updaters are likewise driven by EM configuration, and it lives in its own repo too. If reading EM configuration is what makes a daemon belong in entity-manager, that argument pulls in every EM consumer, and entity-manager\u0027s own scope (FRU probe + configuration publishing) stops meaning much.\n\n2. This repo is not only the daemon — it also carries the CCI encode/decode layer and ccitool. A protocol codec and a debug CLI are harder to justify in entity-manager than the daemon alone.\n\n3. The synchronization cost here is much lower than dbus-sensors. In dbus-sensors, every new sensor type requires a matched config+code change. In this design the two kinds of change are separate:\n   - Adding a new CCI-capable device or board is a pure EM configuration change — the MCTP event-interrupt policy is added together with that device\u0027s configuration, and ccid needs no modification at all. \n- Adding support for a new CCI command is a pure ccid change and does not touch EM at all.\n\n   So the matched-pair change that hurts dbus-sensors essentially does not arise here.\n\nThanks for the review.",
